Z||000000||Earne||03-17-2000||04:30 AM||||I thought you guys might like to see a GM prototype I ran into last year.  This GT is still owned and highly protected by GM.  <P> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944029&Sequence=2" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944029&Sequence=2</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944031&Sequence=2" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944031&Sequence=2</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944032&Sequence=2" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944032&Sequence=2</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944033&Sequence=2" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=756835&p=17944033&Sequence=2</A> <P><BR>There is at least one GM Fiero prototype at each Michigan Fiero Fest every year.  The Club has a pretty tight connection.  They trailer them in, trailer them out.  Then back to storage.  I'm asking about the '89 for this year's ...maybe.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Gokart||03-17-2000||05:01 AM||||Cool pics to see and I wouldn't mind having it for keepsake but I really don't like it. Why? I'm still trying to decide. For starters I don't like the buttons on the steering wheel and the car is just TOO LONG! ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Earne||03-17-2000||06:24 AM||||It is just a concept car put together as an exercise.  An engineering idea that wasn't even supposed to be built.  It's part of the Pontiac collection of concepts that include:  the first production Fiero, one of the three actual '84 Indy Pace Cars, one of the three 1.8 liter turbo Fieros (motor from J-car Sunbird), all three concept convertibles, a project car built from a pre-production Fiero by Car Craft for Pontiac, and one of the two 2.9 liter aluminum block, V6 turbo test Fieros.  The 2.9 develops about 235 bhp and will do 0-60 in under 6.2 seconds.  The project was scrapped 'cause conservative GM thought the second fastest production car in the world (at the time, Aston Martin was #1) would be just too B-B-BAD for ordinary folks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||03-17-2000||09:25 AM||||Neat!  Did you happen to get any photo's of the "back seats".  It would be interesting to  see! Also, what engine did it have?  I would have to guess that a car with all that extra weight, would need something a little more powerfull than the normal v-6  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Lewis||03-17-2000||09:44 AM||||Cool!  I think the car looks really smart.||||reg||14
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||03-17-2000||02:36 PM||||Hmm.. It's interesting.. but I don't think I like it. it's too long.. only way a back seat might work in a fiero is to move the engine up front and turn the rear clip into a full hatchback.. and put the back seat in there.<P>but then it wouldn't be a fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||03-17-2000||02:50 PM||||darklord,<P>Your idea reminds me of the Geo Storm mini-wagons. <P>bhooper,<P>I imagine that the 2.8 would still be pushing this little gem.  Why?  Back then it was the GM go to engine.  It was used in the S-10, Cavaliers/Sunbirds, Celebrity/Cutlass, Grand Prix/Cutlass Supreme, Lumina and many more.  Most of these would weigh more than a 4 person Fiero. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||Earne||03-18-2000||07:32 AM||||Bill is right.  There was nothing unusual about the engine, typical 2.8 V6.<P>I thought I had pics of the rear seating but I can't find them.  This Fiero was designed to be a "plus 2" vehicle.  Extra storage, small children or a nimble contortionist only in the rear seats.||||reg||1
